ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE YOUTH WELFARE SUPPORT ACT

CHAPTER 1 GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
This Decree prescribes matters delegated by the Youth Welfare Support Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

CHAPTER 1-2 PREFERENTIAL TREATMENT FOR YOUTH <Added 2015.7.24>

Article 1-2 (Preferential Treatment for Youth)
(1) The facilities whose charges may be waived or reduced under Article 3(1) or (2) of the Youth Welfare Support Act (hereinafter the "Act") are as follows: <Amended 2019.3.19>
1. Means of public transportation under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Act on Promotion and Use of Public Transportation;
2. Facilities available for use by youth under Article 17(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Youth Activity Promotion Act.
(2) Youth eligible for waivers or reductions under Article 3(1) or (2) of the Act shall fall into either of the following categories:
1. Youth aged 9 through 18;
2. Youth older than 18 but no older than 24 who attend schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act.

CHAPTER 2 YOUTH HEALTH PROTECTION

Article 2 (Measures to Promote Youth Health and Improve Physical Fitness)
As measures to promote youth health and improve physical fitness under Article 5(1) of the Act, the State and local governments shall encourage sports competitions involving youth and actively support youth sports club activities, and may provide the expenses needed to hold competitions and conduct club activities within budgetary limits. <Amended 2019.3.19>

Article 3 (Establishment and Dissemination of Youth Health and Physical Fitness Standards)
The Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall establish and disseminate youth health and physical fitness standards under Article 5(2) of the Act and shall establish new standards at intervals no longer than 5 years, taking account of the environments in which youth grow up. <Amended 2025.10.1>

Article 3-2 (Recipients and Methods of Menstrual Product Support)
(1) Under Article 5(3) of the Act, the State and local governments shall provide menstrual products to female youth who are persons in the following categories or members of their households: <Amended 2022.4.19; 2025.10.1>
1. Persons in the next-lowest income class under Article 2, subparagraph 10 of the National Basic Living Security Act;
2. Recipients of livelihood, housing, medical or educational benefits under Article 7 of the National Basic Living Security Act;
3. Persons eligible for support under Articles 5 and 5-2 of the Single-Parent Family Support Act;
4. Other persons whom the Minister of Gender Equality and Family considers to need menstrual product support.
(2) The State and local governments may directly provide menstrual products to female youth under paragraph (1), or provide menstrual product vouchers [certificates stating an amount or quantity, including electronic or magnetic records, enabling use of menstrual products]. <Amended 2022.4.19>
(3) The State and local governments may connect to and use the social security information system under Article 37(2) of the Framework Act on Social Security to determine eligibility and perform other menstrual product support work. <Amended 2022.4.19>
(4) Female youth seeking menstrual products or vouchers under paragraph (2) shall apply to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si, Gun, or Gu (a Gu means an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same applies), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Gender Equality and Family. <Amended 2019.3.19; 2022.4.19; 2025.10.1>
(5) Beyond paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for application procedures, methods and related requirements for support shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Gender Equality and Family. <Amended 2025.10.1>

CHAPTER 3 COMMUNITY INTEGRATED YOUTH SUPPORT SYSTEMS

Article 4 (Composition of Community Integrated Youth Support Systems)
(1) A community integrated youth support system under Article 9(1) of the Act (hereinafter an "integrated support system") shall include all of the following institutions or organizations (hereinafter "mandatory partner institutions"): <Amended 2013.1.28; 2014.6.11; 2015.7.24; 2015.11.18; 2018.10.30; 2019.3.19; 2020.12.31>
1. Youth counseling and welfare centers under Article 29 of the Act and youth welfare facilities under Article 31 of the Act;
2. Youth support facilities under Article 9(1)2 of the Act on Prevention of Commercial Sex Acts and Protection of Victims;
3. Youth organizations under Article 3, subparagraph 8 of the Framework Act on Youth;
4. Local governments under Article 2 of the Local Autonomy Act;
5. Offices of education and district offices of education of Special Metropolitan Cities, Metropolitan Cities, Special Self-Governing Cities, Dos and Special Self-Governing Provinces (hereinafter "Cities and Dos") under the Local Education Autonomy Act;
6. Schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
7. City and Do police agencies and police stations under Article 13 of the Act on the Organization and Operation of National Police and Autonomous Police;
8. Public health and medical institutions under Article 2, subparagraph 3 of the Public Health and Medical Services Act;
9. Public health centers under Article 10 of the Regional Public Health Act (including public health and medical centers; hereinafter the same applies in this Article);
10. Youth delinquency prevention centers under Article 39-2 of the Organization of the Ministry of Justice and Its Affiliated Agencies;
11. Regional employment and labor offices and branch offices under Articles 19 and 23 of the Organization of the Ministry of Employment and Labor and Its Affiliated Agencies;
12. Support centers for out-of-school youth under Article 12(1) of the Act on Support for Out-of-School Youth;
13. Probation offices under Article 14 of the Act on Probation (including branch probation offices; hereinafter the same applies in this Article).
(2) An integrated support system shall carry out the following activities:
1. Support for local resident organizations' activities to identify, protect and support youth in crisis under Article 11 of the Act;
2. Installation and operation of counseling telephone services under Article 12(2) of the Act (hereinafter "counseling telephone services") and related services;
3. Provision of youth counseling, emergency rescue, protection, medical support, educational support, assistance toward self-reliance and other services.
(3) When requested to support youth in crisis in connection with their assigned duties, mandatory partner institutions shall cooperate so that necessary support can be provided as the highest priority.
(4) The specific duties of cooperation of mandatory partner institutions shall be as follows: <Amended 2013.12.4; 2014.6.11; 2015.7.24; 2018.10.30; 2021.9.24>
1. Local governments: measures linking the activities of mandatory partner institutions and promoting cooperation to revitalize the integrated support system;
2. City and Do offices of education and district offices of education: requests for counseling assistance for students in crises such as school violence or school dropout within their jurisdictions, and cooperation to promote counseling in schools;
3. Schools at all levels: requests for counseling assistance when a student is absent or wishes to withdraw because of a crisis such as school violence or difficulty adapting to school, or when professional counseling services are otherwise considered necessary;
4. Youth delinquency prevention centers: cooperation in delinquency prevention education and counseling for youth in crisis;
5. Police authorities: requests for protection when youth in crisis, including out-of-home youth, are found, and cooperation in rescuing youth in crisis requiring emergency rescue;
6. Regional employment and labor authorities: cooperation when vocational training or employment support is requested for youth in crisis;
7. Public health and medical institutions and public health centers: cooperation when medical consultation or treatment support is requested for youth in crisis;
8. Youth welfare facilities and youth support facilities: cooperation in temporary, short-term, or medium- to long-term protection of youth;
9. Support centers for out-of-school youth: cooperation when assistance with duties under Article 12(2) of the Act on Support for Out-of-School Youth is requested for youth in crisis;
10. Probation offices: requests for counseling and welfare support when youth on probation are considered to need professional counseling and welfare services.

Article 5 (Matters for Deliberation by Youth Welfare Deliberation Committees)
A youth welfare deliberation committee under Article 10(1) of the Act (hereinafter a "deliberation committee") shall deliberate on the following matters: <Amended 2019.3.19; 2021.9.24>
1. Payment of actual expenses, including travel expenses, to families and guardians of youth in crisis under Article 13(3) of the Act;
2. Selection of youth for special support under Article 15 of the Act;
3. Selection of youth for preventive and restorative protective support under Article 19 of the Act (hereinafter "protective support");
4. Examination of integrated support system operation and measures for its activation;
5. Measures to promote coordinated support for youth in crisis among mandatory partner institutions;
6. Proposals for policies and enactment or amendment of ordinances and rules concerning identification and protection of youth in crisis;
7. Other matters considered necessary to identify and protect youth in crisis that the chairperson of a City or Do or Si, Gun, or Gu deliberation committee submits to a meeting (a Gu means an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same applies).

Article 6 (Installation of Counseling Telephone Services)
(1) The State and local governments shall install counseling telephone services and establish a system for receiving reports concerning youth, providing counseling and coordinating with relevant institutions.
(2) Counseling telephone services shall operate 24 hours a day using the same telephone number nationwide.
(3) The State and local governments shall endeavor to establish youth counseling systems through various information and communications networks, including the Internet and wireless communications.
(4) Matters necessary for installing and operating counseling telephone services and operating information and communications networks, beyond those prescribed in this Decree, shall be determined by the Minister of Gender Equality and Family. <Amended 2025.10.1>

Article 6-2 (Establishment and Operation of the Integrated Information System for Supporting Youth in Crisis)
(1) The Minister of Gender Equality and Family may perform the following duties through the integrated information system for supporting youth in crisis under Article 12-2(1) of the Act (hereinafter the "integrated information system"): <Amended 2025.10.1>
1. Providing information on support for youth in crisis and receiving applications for support;
2. Managing integrated support through coordination of services among institutions supporting youth in crisis;
3. Producing and managing statistics on support for youth in crisis;
4. Other duties the Minister of Gender Equality and Family considers necessary for efficient implementation of support programs for youth in crisis.
(2) The information necessary to perform support work for youth in crisis prescribed by Presidential Decree in Article 12-2(2)16 of the Act means information concerning support for youth in crisis among the following:
1. Information on children covered by surveys for child protection under Article 15-4 of the Child Welfare Act, information entered and managed in the national child abuse information system under Article 28-2(2) of that Act, and information on recipients of integrated services under Article 37(1) of that Act;
2. Information on suicide-related counseling and follow-up care for persons who have attempted suicide provided by suicide prevention centers under Article 13(1) of the Act on Prevention of Suicide and Creation of a Culture of Respect for Life;
3. Information on persons with mental illness managed by heads of mental health welfare centers under Article 15(4) of the Act on the Improvement of Mental Health and the Support for Welfare Services for Mental Patients;
4. Information on social service providers managed through the social service information system under Article 24-2 of the Act on the Use and Provision of Social Security Benefits and Search for Eligible Beneficiaries;
5. Among military service information under Article 77-5(1) of the Military Service Act, information on persons liable for military service assessed as needing professional psychological counseling to promote mental health;
6. Other information agreed with the relevant institutions for performance of mandatory partner institutions' cooperation duties under Article 4(4)1 through 10.

CHAPTER 4 SUPPORT FOR YOUTH IN CRISIS

Article 7 (Details of Special Support for Youth in Crisis)
(1) Special support for youth in crisis under Article 14 of the Act (hereinafter "special support") shall be as follows. However, support under subparagraphs 1 and 2 shall be provided only in cases falling under Article 8(1)3 or 4. <Amended 2013.12.4; 2015.1.6; 2015.9.15; 2015.12.30; 2023.4.18; 2025.10.1>
1. Basic living expenses and food and accommodation support necessary for youth to maintain basic daily needs such as clothing, food and shelter;
2. Costs of health examinations, treatment and other needs for physically and mentally healthy growth;
3. Educational costs needed to continue schooling, including entrance fees and tuition at schools under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and preparation for the middle school or high school graduation equivalency examinations under Articles 97(1)1 and 98(1)1 of its Enforcement Decree;
4. Training expenses needed to improve knowledge, technical skills, practical skills and other abilities for employment;
5. Legal counseling and litigation costs needed by youth facing crises such as violence or abuse;
6. Other costs the Minister of Gender Equality and Family considers necessary for healthy youth development.
(2) Support under paragraph (1) applies only where support with the same content is not being received under other legislation, such as the National Basic Living Security Act, Emergency Aid and Support Act, Medical Care Assistance Act, Social Welfare Services Act, Act on Prevention of Sexual Violence and Protection of Victims, and Act on Prevention of Domestic Violence and Protection of Victims. The Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall determine the specific amounts for each type of support. <Amended 2025.10.1>
(3) Support under paragraph (1) shall last no longer than 1 year and, if necessary, may be extended once for up to 1 year. However, support under paragraph (1)3 and 4 may be extended up to twice.

Article 8 (Selection Criteria for Youth Receiving Special Support)
(1) Youth receiving special support under Article 14 of the Act (hereinafter "youth eligible for special support") shall be selected from among youth in crisis in any of the following categories whose household income is no greater than a specified threshold: <Amended 2015.7.24; 2015.12.30; 2019.3.19; 2021.9.24; 2023.4.18; 2025.11.18>
1. Youth receiving protective support who need assistance to prevent delinquency or deviant behavior;
2. Out-of-school youth under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Act on Support for Out-of-School Youth;
3. Youth without guardians or not effectively receiving protection from their guardians;
4. Youth whose daily lives are substantially impaired because they have almost no interaction with others or have lived only in a limited residential space for a considerable period or longer.
(2) The Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall determine and publicly notify the method of calculating household income and the threshold under paragraph (1), taking account of the types of income-earning activities of household members, household size, budgetary circumstances and other relevant factors. <Added 2015.12.30; amended 2025.10.1>

Article 9 (Selection of Youth Eligible for Special Support)
(1) On receiving a selection application under Article 15(1) of the Act, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si, Gun, or Gu shall, following deliberation by the deliberation committee, decide whether to select the youth and determine the support particulars under Article 7 and related matters within 30 days of the application date. Where necessary to investigate compliance with the selection criteria under Article 8, that period may be extended by up to 14 days. <Amended 2019.3.19; 2021.9.24>
(2) For paragraph (1), the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si, Gun, or Gu shall investigate the following: <Amended 2019.3.19>
1. Matters concerning guardians, including their presence and the degree of protection;
2. Actual living conditions, including the youth's livelihood, schooling and health;
3. Household income and property;
4. Support under other legislation, including the National Basic Living Security Act;
5. Other matters necessary to determine the particulars of support.
(3) Public officials of the Special Self-Governing City, Special Self-Governing Province, or Si, Gun, or Gu conducting an investigation under paragraph (1) shall carry proof of their authority and show it to the persons concerned. <Amended 2019.3.19>
(4) Matters necessary for selection procedures and related matters for youth eligible for special support, beyond paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Gender Equality and Family. <Amended 2021.9.24; 2025.10.1>

Article 10 (Notification of Special Support Decisions)
When a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si, Gun, or Gu decides whether to provide special support to a youth in crisis, the official shall notify the youth, the guardian and the applicant in writing of the substance of the decision, including the particulars, amount and duration of support if support is granted. <Amended 2019.3.19>

CHAPTER 4-2 SUPPORT FOR YOUTH PARENTS <Added 2021.9.24>

Article 10-2 (Family Support Services and Welfare Support for Youth Parents)
(1) "Family support services for youth parents prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-2(1)4 of the Act means:
1. Daily-life assistance services, such as child-rearing guidance and emotional support;
2. Integrated support-management services through linkage of services needed by youth parents.
(2) "Child-rearing cost support, livelihood support, medical support, housing support, youth activity support, and other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-3(2) of the Act means: <Amended 2025.10.1; 2026.6.16>
1. Support for expenses needed by youth parents to protect and raise children under 18;
1-2. Support necessary to maintain basic living, including food, clothing, and shelter, for youth parents and their children;
2. Linkage to medical institutions and counseling services for their health management;
3. Linkage to legal-aid services, including legal counseling and representation, needed by youth parents;
4. Other welfare support recognized and publicly notified by the Minister of Gender Equality and Family as necessary for youth parents' growth and stable child-rearing.
(3) Services under Article 18-2(1) and support under Article 18-3(1) of the Act target youth parents not receiving the same support under other statutes, including the National Basic Living Security Act, Emergency Aid and Support Act, Medical Care Assistance Act, Social Welfare Services Act, Child Care Support Act, Infant Care Act, and Early Childhood Education Act.
(4) Eligible persons shall be selected from those whose household income is no more than a specified percentage of standard median income under Article 2, subparagraph 11 of the National Basic Living Security Act, considering assets and employment status. The percentage shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Gender Equality and Family. <Amended 2025.10.1>
(5) The support period shall be one year and may, if necessary, be extended as determined and publicly notified by the Minister. <Amended 2025.10.1; 2026.6.16>

Article 10-3 (Scope of Financial Information)
(1) "Average deposit balances and other data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-4(1)1 of the Act means:
1. Demand deposits, including ordinary deposits, savings deposits, free savings deposits, and foreign-currency deposits: Average balance during the most recent three months;
2. Savings deposits, including time deposits, installment savings, and regular savings: Deposit balance or total amount paid;
3. Shares, beneficiary certificates, contributions, equity interests, and real-estate (pension) trusts: Latest market value. Article 54(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Inheritance Tax and Gift Tax Act applies mutatis mutandis to valuation of unlisted shares;
4. Bonds, bills, checks, certificates of indebtedness, stock-warrant certificates, and negotiable certificates of deposit: Face value;
5. Pension savings: Regularly paid amount or latest balance;
6. Interest, dividends, or discounts arising from financial assets in subparagraphs 1 through 5.
(2) "Debts and other data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-4(1)2 of the Act means:
1. Loan status and overdue-payment details;
2. Unsettled credit-card balances.
(3) "Data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-4(1)3 of the Act means:
1. Insurance policies: Surrender value or insurance proceeds paid within the most recent year;
2. Pension insurance: Surrender value or regularly paid amounts;
3. Interest or dividends arising from insurance policies and pension insurance under subparagraphs 1 and 2.

Article 10-4 (Provision of Financial Information)
(1) When requesting financial, credit, and insurance information under Article 18-4(1)1 through 3 of the Act (hereinafter "financial information, etc.") pursuant to Article 18-4(2) and (3), the Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall specify the following to the heads of financial companies, etc. under Article 2, subparagraph 1 of the Act on Real Name Financial Transactions and Confidentiality and credit information concentration institutions under Article 2, subparagraph 6 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act (hereinafter "financial institutions, etc."):
1. The name and resident registration number of the recipient of welfare support under Article 18-3(1) of the Act;
2. The scope of information requested, the reference date, and the inquiry period.
(2) Heads receiving such requests shall provide the Minister with financial information, etc. containing:
1. The recipient's name and resident registration number;
2. The name of the providing financial institution, etc.;
3. The names of the relevant financial products and account numbers;
4. The financial information, etc.
(3) The Minister may request provision through information networks of associations, federations, central organizations, or similar bodies to which the financial institutions, etc. belong.

Article 10-5 (Educational Support for Youth Parents)
Persons eligible for educational expenses and equivalency-examination support under Article 18-5(1)1 and 2 of the Act shall be selected from those whose household income is no more than a specified percentage of standard median income under Article 2, subparagraph 11 of the National Basic Living Security Act, considering assets and employment status. The Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall determine and publicly notify that percentage. Where a local government provides support, it may set a different percentage by ordinance considering local circumstances. <Amended 2025.3.25; 2025.10.1; 2026.6.16>

CHAPTER 5 YOUTH PROTECTIVE SUPPORT <Amended 2019.3.19>

Article 11 (Details of Protective Support)
(1) Before providing protective support under Article 19 of the Act, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of a Si, Gun, or Gu shall consult the youth concerned and, where a protective support guardian under Article 21(1) of the Act has been designated, hear that guardian's opinion, and determine the particulars, methods and duration of support. For a youth attending a school under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, the support shall not hinder completion of the school curriculum. <Amended 2019.3.19>
(2) The methods prescribed by Presidential Decree, including counseling, education, volunteering, training, sports and group activities, in Article 19(2) of the Act mean the following: <Added 2019.3.19; amended 2025.10.1>
1. Support for individual counseling, family and guardian counseling, and group counseling;
2. Support for character education, employment training, career education, health education and similar activities;
3. Support for volunteer activities linked to volunteer centers under Article 19(1) of the Framework Act on Volunteer Service Activities or local youth activity promotion centers under Article 7(1) of the Youth Activity Promotion Act;
4. Support for training, cultural, sports and group activities at youth activity facilities under Article 10 of the Youth Activity Promotion Act;
5. Other support the Minister of Gender Equality and Family considers necessary to prevent youth delinquency and deviant behavior and assist return and adaptation to family, school and social life.

CHAPTER 6 YOUTH WELFARE SUPPORT INSTITUTIONS

Article 12 (Business Plans)
The business plan submitted to the Minister of Gender Equality and Family under Article 24(1) of the Act by the Korea Youth Counseling and Welfare Institute under Article 22(1) of the Act (hereinafter the "Youth Counseling Institute") shall include the following: <Amended 2025.10.1>
1. An overview of the activities, including objectives, implementation policies and principal activities;
2. The budget needed to implement the activities and the composition of funding sources;
3. Where government subsidies are sought, the amount and planned uses;
4. Expected effects of implementing the activities;
5. Other matters the Minister of Gender Equality and Family specifies as necessary to examine the appropriateness of the business plan.

Article 13 (Statements of Revenue and Expenditure Accounts)
The statement of revenue and expenditure accounts submitted by the Youth Counseling Institute to the Minister of Gender Equality and Family under Article 24(2) of the Act shall be accompanied by the following documents: <Amended 2025.10.1>
1. A document comparing the business plan for the business year with actual implementation;
2. Audit opinions of the Youth Counseling Institute's auditor and of a certified public accountant (including an accounting corporation);
3. Other material the Minister of Gender Equality and Family specifies for verifying the contents of the accounts. <Amended 2025.10.1>

Article 14 (Establishment of Youth Counseling and Welfare Centers)
(1) A youth counseling and welfare center established under Article 29(1) of the Act (hereinafter a "youth counseling and welfare center") shall perform the following functions: <Amended 2019.3.19>
1. Counseling and welfare support for youth and parents;
2. Development and operation of counseling and welfare programs;
3. Education and training for volunteer counselors and youth leaders under Article 3, subparagraph 7 of the Framework Act on Youth;
4. Operation of telephone services for youth counseling or emergency rescue;
5. Emergency rescue, legal and medical assistance, and temporary protection for youth harmed by youth violence, abuse or similar acts;
6. Support for self-help and rehabilitation to improve youth's ability to live independently;
7. Other activities the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor considers necessary for youth counseling and welfare support.
(2) A City or Do youth counseling and welfare center shall have 1 head and staff performing management, practical work with youth, and general administration; where temporary protection facilities are installed, it shall also have staff providing daily living guidance.
(3) A Si, Gun, or Gu youth counseling and welfare center shall have 1 head and staff performing management and practical work with youth.
(4) Standards for establishment and operation of youth counseling and welfare centers are set out in attached Table 1, and staff qualifications in attached Table 2.
(5) Matters necessary for establishment and operation of youth counseling and welfare centers beyond paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government. <Added 2022.4.19>

Article 15 (Duties of Support Centers for Youth with a Migrant Background)
A support center for youth with a migrant background under Article 30(1) of the Act (hereinafter a "support center for youth with a migrant background") shall perform the following duties:
1. Comprehensive information on welfare for youth with a migrant background;
2. Counseling and education for youth with a migrant background and their parents;
3. Development and training of personnel to support youth with a migrant background;
4. Activities to help the public correctly understand youth with a migrant background;
5. Surveys and research on the actual circumstances of youth with a migrant background;
6. Development and dissemination of programs to assist their social adaptation;
7. Other duties necessary to fulfill the purposes of a support center for youth with a migrant background.

CHAPTER 7 YOUTH WELFARE FACILITIES

Article 16 (Insurance for Youth Welfare Facilities)
Under Article 32(5) of the Act, a person establishing and operating a youth welfare facility shall obtain insurance with insured amounts no lower than those specified below. The insurance payment shall equal actual loss; however, in the case of death, if actual loss is less than 20 million won, the payment shall be 20 million won. <Amended 2019.3.19; 2023.4.18>
1. Death: 150 million won;
2. Injury: the amount under attached Table 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Youth Activity Promotion Act;
3. Physical disability resulting from an injury after treatment of the injury is completed (hereinafter "residual disability"): the amount under attached Table 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Youth Activity Promotion Act;
4. Death during treatment caused by the injury: the sum of the amounts under subparagraphs 1 and 2;
5. Residual disability caused by the injury: the sum of the amounts under subparagraphs 2 and 3;
6. Death caused by the injury after the amount under subparagraph 3 has been paid: the amount under subparagraph 1 less the amount paid under subparagraph 3.

Article 17 (Establishment of Youth Welfare Facilities)
Standards for establishment and operation of youth welfare facilities under Article 32(1) and (2) of the Act shall be as set out in attached Table 3. <Amended 2019.3.19>

Article 17-2 (Exceptions to Notification of Admission to Youth Shelters)
"Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 32-2(2)2 of the Act means:
1. The guardian's contact details are unknown;
2. The guardian cannot be reached because the guardian refuses to receive communications, is detained or admitted to a correctional institution or treatment/protection facility, or for similar reasons.

Article 17-3 (Support for Self-Reliance)
(1) The matters prescribed by Presidential Decree in Article 32-3(1)5 of the Act mean the following: <Amended 2025.10.1>
1. Development and operation of education, training and other programs to strengthen out-of-home youth's capacity for self-reliance;
2. Education and training on support for out-of-home youth's self-reliance for staff of youth welfare facilities under Article 31 of the Act (excluding youth treatment and rehabilitation centers; hereinafter the same applies in this Article);
3. Other matters the Minister of Gender Equality and Family considers necessary to support out-of-home youth's self-reliance.
(2) Out-of-home youth needing support for self-reliance under Article 32-3(1) of the Act shall include persons admitted to and using youth welfare facilities and persons who have left them.

CHAPTER 8 SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 18 (Processing of Sensitive and Personally Identifying Information)
Where unavoidable to perform the following duties, the Minister of Gender Equality and Family and heads of local governments (including persons entrusted with their work under Articles 6(3), 12-2(8), 16(4) and 42-2(1) of the Act) may process material containing health information under Article 23 of the Personal Information Protection Act, information constituting criminal history records under Article 18, subparagraph 2 of its Enforcement Decree, and resident registration numbers, passport numbers or alien registration numbers under Article 19, subparagraphs 1, 2 or 4 of that Decree: <Amended 2013.12.4; 2018.6.5; 2019.3.19; 2021.9.24; 2022.4.19; 2025.10.1>
1. Applications for issuance and reissuance of, and delivery of, youth cards under Article 4 of the Act;
1-2. Provision of menstrual products and related work under Article 5(3) of the Act;
2. Conduct of physical fitness tests and health examinations, notification of results, treatment and related work under Article 6 of the Act;
3. Expert counseling for youth under Article 12 of the Act;
3-2. Establishment and operation of the integrated information system and related work under Article 12-2 of the Act;
4. Counseling and education for families and guardians of youth in crisis under Article 13 of the Act;
5. Special support for youth in crisis and selection of recipients under Article 14 of the Act;
6. Prevention of youth becoming out-of-home youth, and their protection and support, under Article 16 of the Act;
7. Deleted <2015.5.28>
8. Counseling, education and related work to improve social adaptation and learning abilities of youth with a migrant background under Article 18 of the Act;
9. Selection of recipients of protective support, provision of protective support and related work under Article 19 of the Act.

Article 19 (Entrustment of Work)
(1) Pursuant to Article 42(2) of the Act, the Minister of Gender Equality and Family may entrust youth organizations under Article 3, subparagraph 8 of the Framework Act on Youth with work concerning establishment and dissemination of youth health and physical fitness standards under Article 5(2) of the Act. <Amended 2013.12.4; 2018.6.5; 2025.10.1>
(2) When work is entrusted under paragraph (1), the Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall publicly notify its particulars. <Amended 2025.10.1>

Article 19-2 (Review of Regulations)
The Minister of Gender Equality and Family shall review the appropriateness of the standards for establishment and operation of youth welfare facilities under Article 17 and attached Table 3 every 3 years, taking January 1, 2025 as the reference date (meaning before January 1 of every third year), and shall take improvement and other measures. <Amended 2025.10.1; 2025.11.18>

CHAPTER 9 PENALTIES

Article 20 (Standards for Imposing Administrative Fines)
Standards for imposing administrative fines under Article 45 of the Act shall be as set out in attached Table 4.

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 24002, 2012.7.31>
Article 1 (Enforcement Date) This Decree enters into force on August 2, 2012.
Article 2 (Transitional Measures on Youth Counseling and Welfare Centers)
City/Do youth counseling and emergency rescue institutions and Si/Gun/Gu youth support institutions deemed centers under Article 4 of the Addenda to Act No. 11290 wholly amending the Youth Welfare Support Act shall operate under previous establishment/operation and personnel qualification standards, but shall comply with amended attached Tables 1 and 2 within three years after enforcement.
Article 3 (Transitional Measures on Facility Establishment and Operation Standards)
