Standards for Establishment and Operation of Youth Counseling and Welfare Centers (Article 14(4))

ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE YOUTH WELFARE SUPPORT ACT [Attached Table 1] <Amended 2025.10.1>
STANDARDS FOR ESTABLISHMENT AND OPERATION OF YOUTH COUNSELING AND WELFARE CENTERS (Article 14(4))
1. Location
The location must have sufficient sunlight, lighting and ventilation, water supply and transportation, enabling maintenance of staff and users' health, disaster prevention, and suitability for daily life.
2. Structures and Equipment
(a) A center must have:
1) At least one office.
2) At least one counseling waiting room, with facilities for reading books and other materials.
3) At least one group-counseling and psychological-testing room (the two uses may share a room).
4) At least one telephone and other counseling room (telephone counseling and counseling through other information networks may share a room).
5) Sanitary facilities: Toilets and washrooms suitable for the numbers of staff and anticipated users.
6) Program rooms: Separate spaces equal in number to the programs implemented.
7) At least one education room for youth, guardians, and others.
8) Individual counseling rooms:
(a) City/Do centers: (1) At least five rooms; (2) Separate spaces or soundproofing for smooth counseling and privacy.
(b) Si/Gun/Gu centers: (1) At least three rooms; (2) Separate spaces or soundproofing for smooth counseling and privacy.
9) Temporary protection accommodation (City/Do centers):
(1) At least one separate accommodation each for males and females.
(2) At least one each of sleeping quarters, washroom, cooking facilities, office, and counseling room.
(3) Telephone, firefighting equipment, etc.
(b) Size:
1) City/Do center: Independent space of at least 400 m² total floor area.
2) Si/Gun/Gu center: Independent space of at least 200 m² total floor area.
3. Operation
(a) City/Do centers operate with at least 14 staff and Si/Gun/Gu centers with at least five; flexible operation according to local circumstances is permitted. In such cases, City/Do centers must employ at least three youth counselors and Si/Gun/Gu centers at least one.
(b) The center head must serve full-time and may not also perform another institution's duties, except:
1) A head of a center directly established and operated under Article 29(1) of the Act by a Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Do Governor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of Si/Gun/Gu;
2) A center head also heading another institution designated by the Minister of Gender Equality and Family, where concurrent duties are necessary considering their relationship and efficiency.
Qualification Standards for Youth Counseling and Welfare Center Personnel (Article 14(4))

ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE YOUTH WELFARE SUPPORT ACT [Attached Table 2] <Amended 2025.10.1>
QUALIFICATIONS FOR YOUTH COUNSELING AND WELFARE CENTER PERSONNEL (Article 14(4))
1. City/Do Centers
Category | Qualification standards
(a) Center head:
1) Both: (a) Doctorate or completion of a doctoral course in counseling/welfare; (b) At least three years' practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
2) Both: (a) Master's degree in counseling/welfare; (b) At least five years' such experience.
3) Both: (a) Graduation from a four-year university in counseling/welfare, or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least seven years' such experience.
4) At least five years' such experience, recommendation by a specialized institution or organization as possessing ability and aptitude in youth counseling/welfare, and recognition by the Mayor/Do Governor.
(b) Management staff:
1) Both: (a) Doctorate or completion of a doctoral course in counseling/welfare; (b) At least one year's practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
2) Both: (a) Master's degree in counseling/welfare; (b) At least three years' such experience.
3) Grade 1 youth counselor.
4) At least three years performing practical youth-oriented duties at a youth counseling/welfare center.
5) Grade 1 mental-health clinical psychologist under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Improvement of Mental Health and the Support for Welfare Services for Mental Patients (hereinafter a "mental-health clinical psychologist").
6) Clinical psychology specialist qualification conferred by an organization or academic society designated by the Minister of Gender Equality and Family among those established to promote public psychological health.
(c) Staff performing practical youth-oriented duties:
1) Master's degree or higher in counseling/welfare and recognition by the Mayor/Do Governor as capable of youth counseling/welfare duties.
2) Both: (a) Four-year university graduation in counseling/welfare or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least one year's practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
3) Both: (a) Four-year university graduation or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least three years' such experience.
4) Grade 2 or higher youth counselor.
5) Grade 2 or higher mental-health clinical psychologist.
6) Grade 1 clinical psychologist under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Technical Qualifications Act.
7) Both: (a) Grade 3 youth counselor, Grade 2 youth instructor, or Grade 2 or higher social worker; (b) At least two years' practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
8) Clinical psychology specialist qualification conferred by a minister-designated organization or academic society established to promote public psychological health.
(d) Administrative staff:
Junior college graduation or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute, and recognition by the Mayor/Do Governor as capable of administrative duties.
(e) Daily-life guidance staff:
1) Bachelor's degree or higher in counseling/welfare and recognition by the Mayor/Do Governor as capable of youth counseling/welfare duties.
2) Both: (a) Junior college graduation or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least one year's practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
3) Both: (a) Qualification as a youth counselor, youth instructor, or social worker; (b) At least one year's such experience.
2. Si/Gun/Gu Centers
(a) Center head:
1) Both: (a) Doctorate or completion of a doctoral course in counseling/welfare; (b) At least one year's practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
2) Both: (a) Master's degree in counseling/welfare; (b) At least three years' such experience.
3) Both: (a) Four-year university graduation in counseling/welfare or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least five years' such experience.
4) Grade 1 youth counselor.
5) At least three years' such experience, recommendation by a specialized institution or organization as possessing ability and aptitude in youth counseling/welfare, and recognition by the head of Si/Gun/Gu.
(b) Management staff:
1) Doctorate or completion of a doctoral course in counseling/welfare.
2) Both: (a) Master's degree or higher in counseling/welfare; (b) At least two years' practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
3) Grade 2 or higher youth counselor.
4) At least three years performing practical youth-oriented duties at a youth counseling/welfare center.
(c) Staff performing practical youth-oriented duties:
1) Master's degree or higher in counseling/welfare and recognition by the head of Si/Gun/Gu as capable of youth counseling/welfare duties.
2) Both: (a) Four-year university graduation in counseling/welfare or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least six months' practical youth counseling/welfare experience.
3) Both: (a) Junior college graduation in counseling/welfare or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least three years' such experience.
4) Both: (a) Four-year university graduation or equivalent or higher education recognized under another statute; (b) At least two years' such experience.
5) Grade 2 or higher youth counselor.
6) Both: (a) Grade 3 youth counselor, Grade 2 youth instructor, or Grade 2 social worker; (b) At least one year's such experience.
Notes:
Terms in sections 1 and 2:
1. "Counseling/welfare field" means counseling, education, psychology, social welfare (social work), youth (guidance) studies, and fields recognized by the Mayor/Do Governor as related to counseling/welfare.
2. "Practical youth counseling/welfare experience" means:
(a) Performing counseling/welfare duties for youth as a regular employee at a youth-related institution or organization;
(b) Performing youth-policy duties as a regular employee in a youth-related department of a central administrative agency or local government.
